 ## 2026-11-17
-- **Fixed:** Streamer clip picking no longer puts the loudest game moment first when the streamer didn't react to it. Several changes work together:
-  - **Mic on its own channel:** when the recording has the mic on one side and the game on the other, the app listens to the mic alone.
-  - **Screams count:** a scream counts as a reaction even when it isn't much louder than the streamer's normal talk.
-  - **Face measured itself:** the facecam's movement is measured on the face, not the room behind it.
-  - **Reaction and gameplay together:** a game event only ranks high when the streamer actually responds to it.
-
-  On the reference test video the scream now ranks first and the loud explosion with no reaction is left out. Set `REACTION_FUSION=0`, `REACTION_MIC_CHANNEL=0` or `REACTION_FACE_ACTIVITY=roi` to switch the parts off.
-- **Under the hood:** Streamer moments now also carry the reaction contract's fields. These are a 0–1 strength for each signal (facecam reaction, voice burst, emotional words, gameplay event; empty when not measured, never a fake zero), a fused 0–100 score, a one-sentence reason, and a layout recommendation. Existing fields are unchanged. Set `REACTION_CONTRACT_FIELDS=0` to leave them out.
-- **New:** Two "Face on top, game below" layouts for streamer clips, in the style of popular gaming pages. The streamer's facecam fills the top of the frame, cropped tight, and the gameplay fills the bottom. The captions sit on the seam a couple of punchy words at a time. A small "credit @creator" line can go at the bottom. **Red** has a red divider and white captions with a red outline; **Gold** has a gold divider and the spoken word in yellow. Pick one under "Gaming layout" on the create page when the niche is Gaming, and optionally type the creator's name for the credit. Clips without a facecam keep the normal layout.
-- **Improved:** Streamer clips now find the facecam even when it sits half-way down the side of the screen or the face is small, and even when the game shows no faces. The app looks for the camera window itself, checks there is a face in it, and then puts the streamer on top and the game below. It ignores video calls (several people in fixed windows) and stream overlays with a printed face. Set `FACECAM_RECT_DETECT=0` to switch this off.
-- **Fixed:** Video-call podcasts (each person in their own window) sometimes came out as the whole call shrunk into the middle of the vertical frame, with small faces. A still face in a top corner was being mistaken for a streamer's facecam. Now a facecam also needs a camera frame the app can actually measure; otherwise the clip gets the normal layout, which shows both people large, one above the other. Set `REFRAME_REACTOR_NEEDS_INSET=0` to restore the previous check.
-- **Fixed:** On a computer with an NVIDIA graphics card but without the CUDA libraries installed (common outside the Docker image), transcription crashed or hung. It now switches to the processor once and remembers that for the rest of the run. `WHISPER_DEVICE=cpu` forces the processor.
-- **Improved:** Clip quality checks can now flag a clip that is far from the -14 LUFS loudness short-form platforms use, and a clip that opens or ends mid-sentence or cuts a word. Both are warnings only and run when the pipeline supplies the loudness target and transcript.
-- **Improved:** Talking videos now get roughly twice the stock B-roll, placed where it helps. Finance, podcast, education, vlog, travel and true-crime clips plan cutaways at 3–4.5 per minute instead of about one per clip, and each cutaway is chosen from what the speaker is actually saying ("interest rates" shows a central bank, not a random building). Words used as names ("Tim Cook", "Steve Jobs") and passing mentions no longer trigger footage. While the speaker says "look at this" the original picture stays on screen, and during a live reaction (laughing, "wow!") the stock shot appears as a framed picture-in-picture so the face stays visible. A stock search that fails because of a rejected key is now recorded as a provider error instead of "nothing found". Set `BROLL_CADENCE=legacy` to keep the previous behaviour.
-- **Improved:** Streamer reaction picking now also reads what the streamer says — exclamations like "no, no, no", "what!" or "bro" in English, Spanish and romanised Hindi — and checks the facecam mouth so in-game character dialogue is less likely to be mistaken for the streamer. Words alone never make a clip: they must line up with a facecam or voice reaction.
-- **Fixed:** The second NVIDIA fallback for AI clip selection pointed at DeepSeek V4.1 Flash, which NVIDIA has retired (a live check on 17 November got "gone"), so that fallback step always failed. It now uses Nemotron 3.5 Super, which the same check confirmed working on the free NVIDIA tier. The fallback order is unchanged and no paid model was added.
-- **New:** Streamer reaction clips now come with a hook title that describes what actually happened (for example a streamer laughing at a sudden on-screen blast, never an invented event), clip windows that start on a pause and end after the reaction has died down, caption timing and placement notes that follow the reaction and keep clear of the facecam, and editing suggestions (facecam panel, a gentle punch-in on the reaction, trimming dead time). The suggestions are advisory data for the renderer.
-- **New:** Streamer videos now get their best moments from the streamer's reaction, not just the loudest or most explosive stretch. The picker listens for a raised, shouting or laughing voice (ignoring explosions and engine noise), watches the facecam, and matches each reaction to the on-screen event that caused it. A reaction to something that just happened ranks highest, a funny moment with no visible trigger comes next, and a game event the streamer ignored is no longer picked. Each clip keeps the trigger and the full reaction, and explains why. Set `REACTION_SIGNALS=0` to switch the reaction hints off.
-- **Improved:** Gameplay and other no-speech videos now get their best moments from what happens on screen, not just from the loudest sound. The picker watches for several visible changes at once — flashes, banners, on-screen events, sustained movement, screen shake — keeps the build-up before the action and ends after the payoff, and explains each pick. A loud sound with nothing visible happening (like a distant explosion) no longer outranks a real moment. Set `FRAME_EVIDENCE_SELECTION=0` to switch back to the older sound-and-scene-cut picker.
